(3038002, 417922, 3032, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by SandyP:</b></div><hr/><i> Ian, what a cool photo!  So sorry for the dq.  Can''t figure out exactly why, but it really is an awesome photo.  The subject, the focus and lighting are so cool! </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Because the lower part of the image (the woman and the bags) is a billboard at Heathrow airport. That is someone else''s photo/artwork. The only addition by Artan is the upper part with the wall and the processing.</td>', 'Originally posted by SandyP: Ian, what a cool photo!  So sorry for the dq.  Can''t figure out exactly why, but it really is an awesome photo.  The subject, the focus and lighting are so cool!   Because the lower part of the image (the woman and the bags) is a billboard at Heathrow airport. That is someone else''s photo/artwork. The only addition by Artan is the upper part with the wall and the processing.', '2006-11-16 14:09:07.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(3038073, 413909, 41337, '<td valign="top">Very interesting image. However, there is a bit of a disconnect with the viewer. The way you presented this is very bold (i.e. bright lighting, strong contrast, etc), which doesn''t seem to go well with "tranquility" as a theme. The background that you removed could have possibly helped this shot greatly like how it does with many of your other self portrait shots. I agree with taterbug in that the lighting should be more subdued, soft and delicate. Those things would have helped put the viewer in the frame of mind to see tranquility and the essense of this shot. <br/> <br/>Let me add, I think your work in general is fantastic. You really have a great eye for shapes and form and you seem to know exactly what to include and what not to include.</td>', 'Very interesting image. However, there is a bit of a disconnect with the viewer. The way you presented this is very bold (i.e. bright lighting, strong contrast, etc), which doesn''t seem to go well with "tranquility" as a theme. The background that you removed could have possibly helped this shot greatly like how it does with many of your other self portrait shots. I agree with taterbug in that the lighting should be more subdued, soft and delicate. Those things would have helped put the viewer in the frame of mind to see tranquility and the essense of this shot.  Let me add, I think your work in general is fantastic. You really have a great eye for shapes and form and you seem to know exactly what to include and what not to include.', '2006-11-16 14:27:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(3039198, 416191, 18639, '<td valign="top">You got some dust on yo sensor!</td>', 'You got some dust on yo sensor!', '2006-11-17 00:18:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(3039962, 420589, 65886, '<td valign="top">oh wow!:)) I am usually more into "artistic" studio photos than real life but this one is an absolutely wonderful portrait! really, I have never seen such a fantastic expression. what a great capture!</td>', 'oh wow!:)) I am usually more into "artistic" studio photos than real life but this one is an absolutely wonderful portrait! really, I have never seen such a fantastic expression. what a great capture!', '2006-11-17 10:47:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(3040020, 419502, 38127, '<td valign="top">I enjoy it very much... great capture :) and WOW.. what a view you had access to ;)</td>', 'I enjoy it very much... great capture :) and WOW.. what a view you had access to ;)', '2006-11-17 11:21:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(3040173, 407372, 68682, '<td valign="top">I''ve been shooting jockeys for about 4 years in hte NYC area, and this is one of the best racing shots I''ve ever seen. I particularly like that it''s a turf race... but the action is flawless, the lighting is beautiful, and the composition is exquisite as well. truly outstanding.</td>', 'I''ve been shooting jockeys for about 4 years in hte NYC area, and this is one of the best racing shots I''ve ever seen. I particularly like that it''s a turf race... but the action is flawless, the lighting is beautiful, and the composition is exquisite as well. truly outstanding.', '2006-11-17 12:38:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(3040510, 407143, 48717, '<td valign="top">You should try to re-create this one for the post card challenge!  Very nice.</td>', 'You should try to re-create this one for the post card challenge!  Very nice.', '2006-11-17 15:29:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(3041107, 420974, 36382, '<td valign="top">This is beautiful, and now I am even more impressed that you did it without any fancy equipment.</td>', 'This is beautiful, and now I am even more impressed that you did it without any fancy equipment.', '2006-11-17 20:09:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(3041413, 417259, 60862, '<td valign="top">Just an awesome shot.  I love how you got the explosions at the top and the trail from the one about to go off.  Makes me want to sit here for a second to see it blow!  I also love how the sky changes color.</td>', 'Just an awesome shot.  I love how you got the explosions at the top and the trail from the one about to go off.  Makes me want to sit here for a second to see it blow!  I also love how the sky changes color.', '2006-11-18 00:33:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(3042858, 420728, 31637, '<td valign="top">nice eye you have</td>', 'nice eye you have', '2006-11-18 23:32:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(3048025, 419792, 66060, '<td valign="top">I understand that omega-3 pills are good for you, but I, much like the other viewers wonder why the one good pill is better then the others when they all look the same :/ I like the lighting and composition, theme just needs some tweaking :) Keep it up.</td>', 'I understand that omega-3 pills are good for you, but I, much like the other viewers wonder why the one good pill is better then the others when they all look the same :/ I like the lighting and composition, theme just needs some tweaking :) Keep it up.', '2006-11-20 15:13:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(3048261, 414594, 25355, '<td valign="top">That''s a nice green dragonfly. You really need to have sharp focus on the eyes at least, but great capture nevertheless.</td>', 'That''s a nice green dragonfly. You really need to have sharp focus on the eyes at least, but great capture nevertheless.', '2006-11-20 16:22:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(3049214, 407427, 63008, '<td valign="top">Thanks very much for the votes and comments...</td>', 'Thanks very much for the votes and comments...', '2006-11-20 21:43:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(3049515, 420817, 65319, '<td valign="top">What a lovely photo depicting a lush and flowing waterfall.  I can''t help but wonder what this photo would look like with an intensified green in the moss.  The water is just a perfect flowing veil of billowy white.  The  flow of the water is nicely balanced and framed with the fallen log just above it.  Well done as always.</td>', 'What a lovely photo depicting a lush and flowing waterfall.  I can''t help but wonder what this photo would look like with an intensified green in the moss.  The water is just a perfect flowing veil of billowy white.  The  flow of the water is nicely balanced and framed with the fallen log just above it.  Well done as always.', '2006-11-20 23:49:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(3050190, 413909, 60862, '<td valign="top">It''s a nice image.  Don''t worry too much about the DQ - just learn from it.  No one reading what happened will think anything other than "oops - no cheating, just a misunderstanding."  There have been other DQs for the same problem, so you''re not alone.<br/> <br/>At issue is that using your technique, the background in the shot becomes irrelevant.  There''s nothing different between what you produced and cutting the main subject out of any background, no matter how distracting.  That makes composing this more about Photoshop skills and less about using the camera to capture what you want.<br/> <br/>Remember that DPC is explicitly about learning how to shoot well, not just Photoshop well.  Granted, some of the winning images are pretty changed from the original, but again, that''s the idea.<br/> <br/>None of which affects the beauty of this image, as an image in your portfolio outside of a challenge.<br/> <br/>I don''t have a black background, either.  Try shooting in a dark room with lights aimed only at your subject.  I use a few $5 clamp lamps with compact fluorescents rated at the same light output as 150W bulbs.<br/> <br/>Stay away from the walls, and you''ll end up with a bright subject and very dark background.  Increasing the contrast, increasing the black levels (check out layers-&gt;adjustment layer-&gt;selective color), reducing the brightness, etc. will then drop the almost black to all black.<br/> <br/>The exact line of what''s okay to drop out and what''s not is tricky.  I just had an entry where I was worried about it, so I just sent it to the Site Council in advance for an opinion.  The easiest way is to upload both the original and the edit to your workshop, then click help-&gt;contact us-&gt;DPChallenge - General Inquiry and send them the URLs for both.  They try to get back quickly (I heard back within 8 hours), but remember they''re volunteers.</td>', 'It''s a nice image.  Don''t worry too much about the DQ - just learn from it.  No one reading what happened will think anything other than "oops - no cheating, just a misunderstanding."  There have been other DQs for the same problem, so you''re not alone. At issue is that using your technique, the background in the shot becomes irrelevant.  There''s nothing different between what you produced and cutting the main subject out of any background, no matter how distracting.  That makes composing this more about Photoshop skills and less about using the camera to capture what you want. Remember that DPC is explicitly about learning how to shoot well, not just Photoshop well.  Granted, some of the winning images are pretty changed from the original, but again, that''s the idea. None of which affects the beauty of this image, as an image in your portfolio outside of a challenge. I don''t have a black background, either.  Try shooting in a dark room with lights aimed only at your subject.  I use a few $5 clamp lamps with compact fluorescents rated at the same light output as 150W bulbs. Stay away from the walls, and you''ll end up with a bright subject and very dark background.  Increasing the contrast, increasing the black levels (check out layers->adjustment layer->selective color), reducing the brightness, etc. will then drop the almost black to all black. The exact line of what''s okay to drop out and what''s not is tricky.  I just had an entry where I was worried about it, so I just sent it to the Site Council in advance for an opinion.  The easiest way is to upload both the original and the edit to your workshop, then click help->contact us->DPChallenge - General Inquiry and send them the URLs for both.  They try to get back quickly (I heard back within 8 hours), but remember they''re volunteers.', '2006-11-21 09:58:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(3051345, 408923, 66668, '<td valign="top">try using a smaller aperture next time so both girls are in focus</td>', 'try using a smaller aperture next time so both girls are in focus', '2006-11-21 17:21:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(3051480, 420013, 31637, '<td valign="top">very nice smooth feel . Sometimes rocks do not need to be sharpened.</td>', 'very nice smooth feel . Sometimes rocks do not need to be sharpened.', '2006-11-21 17:57:38.000000', NULL, 0),
